WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.049 --> 00:00:12.080
IRENE VAN ZEELAND: Thanks for joining us here
today on this lovely or rainy afternoon!

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:16.720
My name is Irene Van Zeeland.

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:22.640
I work for the Holland Dance Festival as head
of education and outreach.

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:31.359
I'm a queer white woman mid 30s, blonde hair,
sitting in front of a bookshelf.

00:00:31.359 --> 00:00:40.120
Today I'll be talking to Kate, who will introduce
herself.

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:44.930
KATE MARSH: My name is Kate.

00:00:44.930 --> 00:00:52.589
I am a white crip woman, I have long brown
hair with a fringe.

00:00:52.589 --> 00:01:00.510
I'm currently sitting in bed, the only room
I can escape my teenagers dog and cat.

00:01:00.510 --> 00:01:02.810
I'm very happy to be here.

00:01:02.810 --> 00:01:09.870
A tiny bit about my background.

00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:17.000
I've worked in so called inclusive dance for
over 20 years now.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:25.410
I'm a choreographer, I'm a researcher so I
work as a research fellow at a university,

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:37.450
and I also facilitate workshops and various
choreographic residencies and all manner of

00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:38.450
things.

00:01:38.450 --> 00:01:45.720
I'm very interested in rethinking leadership
in terms of dance and the arts and disability.

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:53.970
I'm very interested in crip theory and queer
theory and how these things can empower us

00:01:53.970 --> 00:02:02.010
and give a framework to lots of thinking and
practice, amongst other things.

00:02:02.010 --> 00:02:05.660
Just to give you a sense where the conversation
might go.

00:02:05.660 --> 00:02:11.440
IRENE VAN ZEELAND: I think we're exactly here
to talk about you, so that was a nice start.

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:16.350
Thank you very much.

00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:24.010
One thing I want to ask you is what at this
point in time makes your heart beat faster,

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:28.850
what gets you the most excited in this moment?

00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:34.460
You have a long history researching and working
in the field.

00:02:34.460 --> 00:02:37.330
Today what makes you most excited.

00:02:37.330 --> 00:02:41.030
KATE MARSH: Great question.

00:02:41.030 --> 00:02:44.140
I've become hooked on research.

00:02:44.140 --> 00:02:53.110
In the last few years I've become - I enjoy
so much thinking about through my practice

00:02:53.110 --> 00:03:00.520
and other outputs, written or speaking to
other people, really grounding what we do

00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:04.580
as a sector and what we think in inter-research
terms.

00:03:04.580 --> 00:03:07.050
That's important to me.

00:03:07.050 --> 00:03:14.950
I feel passionate about that because crip
or disabled researchers are in a minority

00:03:14.950 --> 00:03:21.830
in dance research, so I'm interested in growing
that.

00:03:21.830 --> 00:03:34.330
On an emotional level, what really fires me
up - I'll age myself now - is the generation

00:03:34.330 --> 00:03:42.870
behind me, when I encounter younger, hungrier,
less apologetic brilliant crip and disabled

00:03:42.870 --> 00:03:46.000
artists.

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:55.221
They're not less polite, because they are
polite, but there is something - yeah they're

00:03:55.221 --> 00:03:57.739
less polite maybe in what they're demanding.

00:03:57.739 --> 00:04:05.950
That's really exciting and invigorating to
be around and be part of, and to get a sense

00:04:05.950 --> 00:04:13.930
of what the future might be, and how those
of us who have done this work for a long time,

00:04:13.930 --> 00:04:16.850
how that fed into younger generations.

00:04:16.850 --> 00:04:23.599
I'm excited to see how they practice differently
and ask different questions.

00:04:23.599 --> 00:04:28.580
IRENE VAN ZEELAND: Nice.

00:04:28.580 --> 00:04:37.879
In the video we sent along with this invitation
you briefly spoke about that as well.

00:04:37.879 --> 00:04:46.009
That is so exciting, this idea of the new
or young generation being unapologetic about

00:04:46.009 --> 00:04:54.770
their place in the field of dance, gender
identity, climate change, many things - here

00:04:54.770 --> 00:04:58.190
let's stick to the dance element.

00:04:58.190 --> 00:05:02.740
I agree that's an exciting development.

00:05:02.740 --> 00:05:07.060
What do you think we can learn from that attitude?

00:05:07.060 --> 00:05:11.870
How can we use that to our advantage?

00:05:11.870 --> 00:05:17.310
What about that inspires you to change also
maybe the way you think?

00:05:17.310 --> 00:05:19.219
About the way you work?

00:05:19.219 --> 00:05:25.479
KATE MARSH: Another great question.

00:05:25.479 --> 00:05:29.789
This is perhaps a strange but practical answer.

00:05:29.789 --> 00:05:38.509
I learn so much from upcoming generations
of crip artists actually around language.

00:05:38.509 --> 00:05:47.379
You'll maybe note it's been recent for me
to lean into identifying as a crip person

00:05:47.379 --> 00:05:48.379
and crip researcher.

00:05:48.379 --> 00:05:53.559
I span a generation where it required such
a lot of explanation.

00:05:53.559 --> 00:05:59.291
I'm learning from the language people use
to describe themselves and their work.

00:05:59.291 --> 00:06:07.690
It's interesting you said the excitement relates
also to gender identity and climate change

00:06:07.690 --> 00:06:21.550
- to me they're aligned, and crip artists
are often engaging.

00:06:21.550 --> 00:06:32.139
We don't have to make work specifically around
disability.

00:06:32.139 --> 00:06:41.339
We can exercise our expertise in other areas
as crip artists.

00:06:41.339 --> 00:06:45.680
That's exciting to me, that I see many, many
crip artists making work just about their

00:06:45.680 --> 00:06:50.729
knowledge of the body, the world or commenting
on something else.

00:06:50.729 --> 00:06:54.909
That's a definite shift.

00:06:54.909 --> 00:07:04.539
I come from a generation, I feel, where as
crip artists or disabled artists there was

00:07:04.539 --> 00:07:07.779
an expectation to conform.

00:07:07.779 --> 00:07:16.090
Lots of the labour of my work has been trying
to not erase my disability, but be in the

00:07:16.090 --> 00:07:17.090
mainstream.

00:07:17.090 --> 00:07:19.759
This was a repetitive thing.

00:07:19.759 --> 00:07:23.659
When you're in the mainstream, if people don't
notice, you've made it.

00:07:23.659 --> 00:07:39.949
I'm learning from younger artists a rejection
of the mainstream, less borrowing from a language

00:07:39.949 --> 00:07:43.530
that exists, but creating a new language that
suits us.

00:07:43.530 --> 00:07:52.379
I feel privileged to be around that and learn
that, and I feed it in to my practice.

00:07:52.379 --> 00:08:03.889
IRENE VAN ZEELAND: If there is any moment
where you want to ask a question, don't hesitate

00:08:03.889 --> 00:08:07.889
to unmute or wave.

00:08:07.889 --> 00:08:10.539
Same for you Leisa.

00:08:10.539 --> 00:08:17.729
Either type in the chat or raise your hand
or unmute.

00:08:17.729 --> 00:08:25.110
JOE TURNBULL: I'm interested in the idea of
inter-generational stuff.

00:08:25.110 --> 00:08:32.529
In my work with disability arts we work with
a lot of stalwarts of the disability arts

00:08:32.529 --> 00:08:40.620
movement who were part of the direct action
network and there in the 70s or 80s, I guess

00:08:40.620 --> 00:08:50.010
a generation before you at least Kate.

00:08:50.010 --> 00:08:57.889
Maybe there's this change - it's gone from
that, which came from the agitprop politicised

00:08:57.889 --> 00:09:06.160
way of making art, maybe it changed, hopefully
it's coming back again, but it seems the understanding

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:16.790
of disability itself is different between
generations, and it's important for that inter-generational

00:09:16.790 --> 00:09:27.480
learning, learning from people who were there
decades ago fighting for rights, and learning

00:09:27.480 --> 00:09:32.970
from the younger generation with fire in the
belly.

00:09:32.970 --> 00:09:43.649
The idea of not reinventing the wheel, and
past work has been done, and there's stuff

00:09:43.649 --> 00:09:47.860
to learn from that as well.

00:09:47.860 --> 00:09:48.860
I'm rambling now.

00:09:48.860 --> 00:09:52.339
IRENE VAN ZEELAND: I feel what you're saying,
it's nice to know whose shoulders you're standing

00:09:52.339 --> 00:10:03.440
on and who got you where you are, but at the
same time not necessarily conforming to that

00:10:03.440 --> 00:10:12.570
as the newer generation - it gives you roots
and a place you come from but doesn't necessarily

00:10:12.570 --> 00:10:23.230
define where you have 
to go.

00:10:23.230 --> 00:10:35.829
KATE MARSH: Just in response Joe, this is
going to be a bit cynical - no, realistic

00:10:35.829 --> 00:10:40.220
- I think you're right.

00:10:40.220 --> 00:10:49.050
If we think back to our cultural or historic
legacy around disability activism, we have

00:10:49.050 --> 00:10:51.699
that to build on.

00:10:51.699 --> 00:10:59.250
We've had a period not connecting to that,
and we're back to a deeply politicised way

00:10:59.250 --> 00:11:09.570
of being - not necessarily just politicised
around disability, it's more complicated than

00:11:09.570 --> 00:11:10.570
that.

00:11:10.570 --> 00:11:22.209
I don't think it's cynical, but I think it's
important, I think that's been forced by seismic

00:11:22.209 --> 00:11:30.740
economic societal shifts that have actually
thrown disabled people back into extreme precarity.

00:11:30.740 --> 00:11:35.490
I think that's what is happening, to some
extent.

00:11:35.490 --> 00:11:45.449
That initial activism came from a place of
limited rights and real inequality, and it's

00:11:45.449 --> 00:11:53.940
depressing but I think in the UK we're shifting
back to that, definitely in the pandemic.

00:11:53.940 --> 00:12:03.819
I know artists who have been made hugely precarious
by government decisions, which forced us to

00:12:03.819 --> 00:12:07.819
go, We're not having this.

00:12:07.819 --> 00:12:15.319
It's almost from a place of having to, not
choosing to.

00:12:15.319 --> 00:12:17.439
Maylis?

00:12:17.439 --> 00:12:23.790
MAYLIS: Hi everyone.

00:12:23.790 --> 00:12:26.390
Really happy to be here and listen to you
Kate.

00:12:26.390 --> 00:12:34.310
It's been a long time - I heard from your
work and listened to a lot of interviews and

00:12:34.310 --> 00:12:39.350
I'm really interested in what you do.

00:12:39.350 --> 00:12:45.330
I'm also a disabled artist based in the French
Basque country.

00:12:45.330 --> 00:12:57.610
I'm in a generation that is a bit in the middle
between your generation and the new generation,

00:12:57.610 --> 00:12:58.610
generation Z.

00:12:58.610 --> 00:13:04.810
I also think there is this shift going on
right now.

00:13:04.810 --> 00:13:11.730
My question is, because I live in France and
maybe disability is not the same, we don't

00:13:11.730 --> 00:13:19.560
talk about disability the same way in France
and the UK, and as a disabled artist I'm really

00:13:19.560 --> 00:13:29.410
struggling sometimes to talk about my work
and what I do in a radical or political way,

00:13:29.410 --> 00:13:35.510
because France is a very politically correct
country.

00:13:35.510 --> 00:13:44.070
When you talk about disability it's about
the social aspect of it.

00:13:44.070 --> 00:13:56.370
When you're in the culture, there is no funding
for inclusive dance as a cultural genre or

00:13:56.370 --> 00:13:58.880
style.

00:13:58.880 --> 00:14:06.939
It's not a question but a comment, is the
new world and new institutions really ready

00:14:06.939 --> 00:14:14.980
for artists like me who are disabled and want
to do things differently, are they really

00:14:14.980 --> 00:14:17.279
ready for us to come?

00:14:17.279 --> 00:14:22.329
And how can we make them ready?

00:14:22.329 --> 00:14:27.910
How can we push for our needs to be considered?

00:14:27.910 --> 00:14:34.300
KATE MARSH: I think that's a really great
observation.

00:14:34.300 --> 00:14:41.379
The first thing that interests me about what
you're saying is how often when we have really

00:14:41.379 --> 00:14:49.189
cemented social or cultural understandings
of disability, however well meant, they can

00:14:49.189 --> 00:14:57.540
make us invisible, and make it difficult to
have fluid or radical conversations because

00:14:57.540 --> 00:15:06.340
as disabled people we become trapped in a
language and understanding that is put on

00:15:06.340 --> 00:15:08.899
us, rather than one we choose.

00:15:08.899 --> 00:15:16.459
Speaking personally I don't have a fixed sense
of my disability.

00:15:16.459 --> 00:15:23.730
I have a fixed sense of my identity as a crip
person and disabled person, but within that

00:15:23.730 --> 00:15:26.230
it changes all the time.

00:15:26.230 --> 00:15:37.540
If someone layers on me, You need this today,
it's un-helpful in progressing and moving

00:15:37.540 --> 00:15:40.129
things forward.

00:15:40.129 --> 00:15:45.670
It's interesting the different geographic
contexts.

00:15:45.670 --> 00:15:52.750
I had a conversation with a friend of mine,
not a dancer but a fluent French speaker who

00:15:52.750 --> 00:15:56.490
lived in Holland many years.

00:15:56.490 --> 00:16:03.449
Her work is around working with migrant cultures.

00:16:03.449 --> 00:16:14.819
She said the same, that in France it's so
bound up it's difficult to have a truly progressive

00:16:14.819 --> 00:16:16.519
conversation.

00:16:16.519 --> 00:16:20.949
Just to say someone else said the same as
you.

00:16:20.949 --> 00:16:23.319
I don't know what the answer is.

00:16:23.319 --> 00:16:36.129
What excites me at the moment, and it's been
a strange twist of Covid, is I think we are

00:16:36.129 --> 00:16:43.160
more connected, we can collectivise as disabled
people now across countries and contexts,

00:16:43.160 --> 00:16:46.779
and I'm already feeling the power in that.

00:16:46.779 --> 00:16:54.939
We've all felt connected to each other but
we can now come into virtual spaces and feel

00:16:54.939 --> 00:17:06.439
supported and have an allyship between us,
rather than depending on non disabled gatekeepers.

00:17:06.439 --> 00:17:13.741
I hope you and other disabled artists can
draw on that, even to go, Argh, this thing

00:17:13.741 --> 00:17:21.040
happened and I'm frustrated.

00:17:21.040 --> 00:17:27.240
Even practical things about writing about
your practice and putting it out to your network,

00:17:27.240 --> 00:17:36.560
peers and crip friends and asking what they
read from the language, we need to capitalise

00:17:36.560 --> 00:17:41.490
that more and lean into each other.

00:17:41.490 --> 00:17:47.030
I really hear how frustrating it must be in
that context.

00:17:47.030 --> 00:17:56.380
You'll all know Dan Daw, a good friend of
mine, and his practice.

00:17:56.380 --> 00:18:00.190
We just worked on his solo show a bit.

00:18:00.190 --> 00:18:05.120
We talked about the impact of being or feeling
like the only one.

00:18:05.120 --> 00:18:10.400
We've probably all experienced that at one
point.

00:18:10.400 --> 00:18:14.480
,It's really hard.

00:18:14.480 --> 00:18:21.640
I'm excited about finding a way to connect
with each other so nobody has the feeling

00:18:21.640 --> 00:18:24.570
they're on their own.

00:18:24.570 --> 00:18:33.340
JOE TURNBULL: In the chat I encourage you
to look at in your own time, it was a great

00:18:33.340 --> 00:18:38.860
event last night, real community, and rediscovering
the feeling from the beginning of lockdown

00:18:38.860 --> 00:18:50.080
where it really felt like we were all together
again.

00:18:50.080 --> 00:18:54.720
KATE MARSH: Was that Gareth and Gemma?

00:18:54.720 --> 00:18:57.060
What a tiny world we're in.

00:18:57.060 --> 00:19:05.230
I feel very proud of them, I did a programme
with the Arts Council called Change Makers

00:19:05.230 --> 00:19:13.380
where we brought together 21 queer crip artists
together to collaborate, so I match-made them.

00:19:13.380 --> 00:19:18.840
JOE TURNBULL: The event was really brilliant,
do catch up with it.

00:19:18.840 --> 00:19:22.910
KATE MARSH: They're super exciting, I love
them.

00:19:22.910 --> 00:19:26.480
JOE TURNBULL: It was really good.

00:19:26.480 --> 00:19:39.100
The thing I wanted to ask about was in the
rush to reopen and all those things, when

00:19:39.100 --> 00:19:46.320
so many in our community are still shielding,
how we navigate that as artists, whether that's

00:19:46.320 --> 00:19:50.320
a conversation we should be having with venues.

00:19:50.320 --> 00:20:02.320
There's that sense of disabled audiences and
performers getting left behind in the rush

00:20:02.320 --> 00:20:10.920
to go back to normal, and whether there is
a role as artists and performers to be having

00:20:10.920 --> 00:20:15.270
that conversation with venues if they're not
making provisions.

00:20:15.270 --> 00:20:27.070
KATE MARSH: I was involved with two projects
in lockdown which I am very proud of.

00:20:27.070 --> 00:20:39.501
First was called In Other Words, a book of
writing, musings, artworks by queer crip and

00:20:39.501 --> 00:20:42.780
artists of colour.

00:20:42.780 --> 00:20:49.860
The first book was around what does it feel
to be hurtling towards a new normal when normal

00:20:49.860 --> 00:20:52.960
didn't mean anything to you in the first place.

00:20:52.960 --> 00:20:58.310
It's beautiful, full of hope and anger.

00:20:58.310 --> 00:21:12.450
The second book asks how are you now we're
in the new normal.

00:21:12.450 --> 00:21:19.140
That's something we can do - when we have
facilities - because I did this with funding

00:21:19.140 --> 00:21:28.200
and an arts organisation called Metal, and
we created space to bring people together

00:21:28.200 --> 00:21:30.490
with their experiences.

00:21:30.490 --> 00:21:37.090
The other slightly drier but important project
I did was Arts Council funded project called

00:21:37.090 --> 00:21:41.930
the [DC44C].

00:21:41.930 --> 00:21:50.300
It was me and 3 other disabled producers having
conversations with disabled people primarily

00:21:50.300 --> 00:21:54.000
about audiences and artists.

00:21:54.000 --> 00:22:02.570
When we asked the question what do you want
when we emerge, people said, please don't

00:22:02.570 --> 00:22:17.230
change streaming, please don't marginalise
us for needing to be virtual, or punish us

00:22:17.230 --> 00:22:18.660
for needing to shield.

00:22:18.660 --> 00:22:21.900
That was loud and clear from everybody.

00:22:21.900 --> 00:22:29.060
I think Joe your concerns are right - I'm
not sure venues in the capitalist model of

00:22:29.060 --> 00:22:37.520
wanting to get back to money and fill auditoriums,
I'm not sure they're thinking about that.

00:22:37.520 --> 00:22:49.440
The pragmatist in me says one solution is
let's get really clear on articulating that,

00:22:49.440 --> 00:22:57.140
in policies, in riders performers put through,
and what we demand as audience members.

00:22:57.140 --> 00:22:58.840
There is interesting stuff happening.

00:22:58.840 --> 00:23:00.700
We need to amplify it so it becomes difficult
for venues not to stream work.

00:23:00.700 --> 00:23:24.770
And also to nod to venues that do do it.

00:23:24.770 --> 00:23:32.270
I work on and off with The Junction in Cambridge
who are brilliant and committed to streaming

00:23:32.270 --> 00:23:37.420
and thinking about how to invite disabled
audience members into their venue and work

00:23:37.420 --> 00:23:39.040
with disabled artists.

00:23:39.040 --> 00:23:43.170
It's good to nod to good practice.

00:23:43.170 --> 00:23:45.790
JOE TURNBULL: Absolutely.

00:23:45.790 --> 00:23:56.180
I wonder if something like shared tools, or
if there's research that demonstrates how

00:23:56.180 --> 00:24:05.620
excluded and left behind people are feeling,
if you include in artists riders that we could

00:24:05.620 --> 00:24:06.700
circulate.

00:24:06.700 --> 00:24:12.910
KATE MARSH: Sometimes we don't know what we
need.

00:24:12.910 --> 00:24:16.200
I had a great conversation with [Cindri Renaud].

00:24:16.200 --> 00:24:26.200
We had a really great conversation.

00:24:26.200 --> 00:24:32.230
They said, I don't know what I need from Monday
to Friday.

00:24:32.230 --> 00:24:33.230
It might change.

00:24:33.230 --> 00:24:39.500
What I actually need from a venue is to understand
that's a changeable thing.

00:24:39.500 --> 00:24:43.790
That's the same from audience members - to
understand we may not just need access - we

00:24:43.790 --> 00:24:56.750
may need access meaning we can come in and
out, or come in late, or sneak out early.

00:24:56.750 --> 00:25:06.400
I think some kind of toolkit or documentation
that articulates that for us would be really

00:25:06.400 --> 00:25:07.400
good.

00:25:07.400 --> 00:25:14.160
JOE TURNBULL: To be clear I don't think that
should fall on artists, it's the responsibility

00:25:14.160 --> 00:25:21.760
of venues, but you get the opportunity sometimes
to have a dialogue with a venue and it's worth

00:25:21.760 --> 00:25:28.770
taking that opportunity if you can, but it's
an extra burden on artists.

00:25:28.770 --> 00:25:36.450
It's how we should make it easy for artists
to broach those conversations.

00:25:36.450 --> 00:25:47.970
IRENE VAN ZEELAND: It links into something
you said in the video we shared about asking

00:25:47.970 --> 00:25:50.260
people for advice and help.

00:25:50.260 --> 00:26:00.170
Thinking about France, I live in France as
well, that's a huge difference between the

00:26:00.170 --> 00:26:04.040
situation in France and the UK.

00:26:04.040 --> 00:26:09.980
Thinking about that, you're advising younger
artists or artists in different countries

00:26:09.980 --> 00:26:24.110
that don't have the same access as experienced
artists like you - 

00:26:24.110 --> 00:26:34.400
what would you What would be some of those
experiences that were crucial for you that

00:26:34.400 --> 00:26:42.110
you want to share, if the person is not necessarily
coming to you, what are the things you feel

00:26:42.110 --> 00:26:45.450
are really worth sharing in terms of experience.

00:26:45.450 --> 00:26:54.200
If you look at for example the French context
where there is so much work to do, it feels

00:26:54.200 --> 00:27:06.400
daunting to see how little there is and how
far it still has to come.

00:27:06.400 --> 00:27:10.490
KATE MARSH: That's a huge question.

00:27:10.490 --> 00:27:20.720
I think that - I actually think I've done
most of my profound learning in much smaller

00:27:20.720 --> 00:27:25.960
contexts, actually when I haven't been trying
to break down a big wall.

00:27:25.960 --> 00:27:33.270
I've done most of my really active learning
when I've been with my collaborator Welly

00:27:33.270 --> 00:27:38.850
in the studio or working with a filmmaker
I work with.

00:27:38.850 --> 00:27:46.770
That's where I'm really learning.

00:27:46.770 --> 00:27:51.400
The reason that's complicated for me is because
I'm really aware I perhaps couldn't have learnt

00:27:51.400 --> 00:27:54.470
that when I'm 20.

00:27:54.470 --> 00:28:02.420
It's difficult to imagine going back and saying
to my 20 year old self, don't be in the mainstream,

00:28:02.420 --> 00:28:04.210
be radical.

00:28:04.210 --> 00:28:13.870
Because I was in a different place and everything
was different.

00:28:13.870 --> 00:28:15.530
I don't know.

00:28:15.530 --> 00:28:20.900
I'm trying to find a moment I'd want people
to know.

00:28:20.900 --> 00:28:29.620
I think I'd want to say to all disabled people
who are in difficult geographic contexts or

00:28:29.620 --> 00:28:38.020
whatever difficult contexts, to lean into,
or just be around the communities that are

00:28:38.020 --> 00:28:45.030
- we're so closely knit as a community, across
countries I think as well.

00:28:45.030 --> 00:28:54.340
I had an interesting meeting with colleagues
in Poland following the Polish lab I did in

00:28:54.340 --> 00:28:55.700
October.

00:28:55.700 --> 00:29:04.090
The British Council says what would you like
next, and they said, maybe to have Kate back,

00:29:04.090 --> 00:29:08.370
and Candoco, and Stopgap, and Clare Cunningham.

00:29:08.370 --> 00:29:22.010
I found myself thinking, is there a space
we can tap into the fact we all know each

00:29:22.010 --> 00:29:30.410
other - so if you've got me, you've got access
to Clare, who is an email or phone call away.

00:29:30.410 --> 00:29:34.460
Same with Maylis.

00:29:34.460 --> 00:29:44.430
We have this conversation, we don't have to
go, Stopgap do this - we all know each other.

00:29:44.430 --> 00:29:52.110
We can share information freely and point
each other - there is enough to go round,

00:29:52.110 --> 00:29:55.530
is what I'm saying.

00:29:55.530 --> 00:29:58.180
That feels really important.

00:29:58.180 --> 00:30:06.190
IRENE VAN ZEELAND: That seems like very solid
advice, maybe not to be afraid to reach out

00:30:06.190 --> 00:30:12.370
to people maybe in another country or maybe
you've only met them once.

00:30:12.370 --> 00:30:20.970
The network is so tightly knit you can find
your way in.

00:30:20.970 --> 00:30:24.530
That touched me you said that, ask us for
advice.

00:30:24.530 --> 00:30:40.710
KATE MARSH: Also to get political, we, the
disabled, the crip community, are repressed,

00:30:40.710 --> 00:30:45.710
are historically oppressed - not repressed
- and are oppressed now and looking at a future

00:30:45.710 --> 00:30:47.130
of oppression.

00:30:47.130 --> 00:30:50.870
That's not all we experience.

00:30:50.870 --> 00:31:00.370
The reason I'm making that comment, is because
if you ask an oppressed person - 

00:31:00.370 --> 00:31:13.220
I'm a crip artist, but I've also gone through
arduous long processes of securing any benefit

00:31:13.220 --> 00:31:17.080
related to my disability.

00:31:17.080 --> 00:31:21.350
I know how that feels and know about objectification.

00:31:21.350 --> 00:31:27.980
I'm not saying it's all about inclusive dance.

00:31:27.980 --> 00:31:40.890
I regularly get objectified or marginalised
on my way to speak or to a performance - that

00:31:40.890 --> 00:31:42.330
happens to me all the time.

00:31:42.330 --> 00:31:49.500
If another disabled person reaches out to
me, of course I want to connect to them.

00:31:49.500 --> 00:31:54.900
We automatically share something, not everything,
but we share this line.

00:31:54.900 --> 00:31:59.160
I'm excited by that line, I don't think we
make enough of it.

00:31:59.160 --> 00:32:08.650
I wrote something recently, I felt it was
important to say.

00:32:08.650 --> 00:32:13.810
We don't automatically understand each other's
experiences, but we can go, I know something

00:32:13.810 --> 00:32:19.730
about where you're at, a little bit of where
you're at.

00:32:19.730 --> 00:32:22.420
That's got to be a good starting point for
a conversation.

00:32:22.420 --> 00:32:26.640
IRENE VAN ZEELAND: That's really beautiful.

00:32:26.640 --> 00:32:32.280
JOE TURNBULL: I had another question, totally
unrelated.

00:32:32.280 --> 00:32:43.890
Just trying to think about people watching
this back, in Beyond Access there are a lot

00:32:43.890 --> 00:32:53.320
of artists whose primary experiences are as
performers, and there are people whose primary

00:32:53.320 --> 00:33:03.380
experience being a performer, moving to be
the creative force is a difficult transition.

00:33:03.380 --> 00:33:17.500
Do you have any advice about how 
you make your own language, how to develop

00:33:17.500 --> 00:33:19.460
that voice.

00:33:19.460 --> 00:33:28.100
How you go from realising someone else's creative
vision to developing your own practice.

00:33:28.100 --> 00:33:35.850
KATE MARSH: That's such an important question,
because it's a transition so many performers

00:33:35.850 --> 00:33:40.160
make.

00:33:40.160 --> 00:33:43.480
I was in Candoco 5 years.

00:33:43.480 --> 00:33:56.010
The way Candoco works is you are a dancer,
a tool, for someone primarily a non disabled

00:33:56.010 --> 00:33:58.270
vision.

00:33:58.270 --> 00:34:05.470
Which is - I'm making no comment around that,
but that's the reality of that situation.

00:34:05.470 --> 00:34:11.830
I was a dancer for somebody else's idea of
what they wanted to say about disability.

00:34:11.830 --> 00:34:24.159
It was a kind of accidental transition to
make work with Welly O Brien, my long term

00:34:24.159 --> 00:34:25.169
friend and collaborator.

00:34:25.169 --> 00:34:30.720
That was an easy transition, because it was
just the two of us, we'd both been in Candoco,

00:34:30.720 --> 00:34:34.270
and were thinking about what we wanted to
say.

00:34:34.270 --> 00:34:42.020
Sometimes we got it wrong, and it might be
different now.

00:34:42.020 --> 00:34:46.570
That was a gentle transition.

00:34:46.570 --> 00:34:54.960
I actually think getting into research is
where I found my voice and language.

00:34:54.960 --> 00:35:03.330
I found it by resisting normative language,
by getting to a point of going, that language

00:35:03.330 --> 00:35:12.890
doesn't work for me, that codified way of
making dance doesn't fit my body.

00:35:12.890 --> 00:35:18.510
I started playing around with this messy language.

00:35:18.510 --> 00:35:25.610
So much of my practice is about mess, in an
unapologetic way.

00:35:25.610 --> 00:35:37.290
I want to welcome that mess and playing with
my practice, my language, and I think I said

00:35:37.290 --> 00:35:45.430
this in the film you ve seen - I'm not always
- physically in life I feel really supported.

00:35:45.430 --> 00:35:54.030
I feel part of a powerful small network of
disabled artists and researchers who I feel

00:35:54.030 --> 00:35:56.360
have got me.

00:35:56.360 --> 00:36:05.910
That gives me the ability, the confidence
to speak - and sorry Joe, this is probably

00:36:05.910 --> 00:36:07.580
more answer than you wanted.

00:36:07.580 --> 00:36:11.130
In my experience, that's what people want
to hear.

00:36:11.130 --> 00:36:20.350
Even in the most highbrow conference, people
want to hear you speak from experience and

00:36:20.350 --> 00:36:21.350
truthfully.

00:36:21.350 --> 00:36:31.220
That doesn't always land well, but I'm stubborn
about doing that.

00:36:31.220 --> 00:36:38.990
I'm not going to read a paper about Foucault
- although I do that occasionally.

00:36:46.326 --> 00:36:48.533
IRENE VAN ZEELAND: I see Leisa you've joined.

00:36:48.533 --> 00:36:52.290
Nice to see you.

00:36:52.290 --> 00:36:58.760
Is there anything you'd like to share or ask
Kate?

00:36:58.760 --> 00:37:07.110
LEISA: Kate thank you, what you said then
really helped me.

00:37:07.110 --> 00:37:15.560
Because I have been doing ensemble work since
I started working with the arts.

00:37:15.560 --> 00:37:25.890
I'm just about, later this year, to start
my first residency to create my own work in

00:37:25.890 --> 00:37:26.890
Australia.

00:37:26.890 --> 00:37:35.480
I must admit to being really scared up until
this point.

00:37:35.480 --> 00:37:50.030
Then hearing you say that it's like, Yeah,
finally I don't have to do, say, move in somebody

00:37:50.030 --> 00:37:52.420
else's curated way.

00:37:52.420 --> 00:37:58.690
You saying, you make it messy, I'm going,
oh it's like a little bit of freedom.

00:37:58.690 --> 00:38:03.540
I can be a hot mess for my first show.

00:38:03.540 --> 00:38:09.460
Because I'm just figuring it all out really,
what I want to say.

00:38:09.460 --> 00:38:20.510
I have the story and idea and vision in my
head, I've probably had it in my head 5-8

00:38:20.510 --> 00:38:23.740
years, finally I get the chance to do it.

00:38:23.740 --> 00:38:36.090
Hearing you say what you just said I'm feeling
more confident it will be a cracker or a show.

00:38:36.090 --> 00:38:43.301
KATE MARSH: Can you see Maylis on screen.

00:38:43.301 --> 00:38:46.430
Did you see her dancing when you were talking?

00:38:46.430 --> 00:38:52.240
That's what I'm talking about, crip power,
you're not on your own.

00:38:52.240 --> 00:38:55.790
Yes, that's good.

00:38:55.790 --> 00:39:01.240
I want to be transparent.

00:39:01.240 --> 00:39:08.700
My position is drop me an email or Facebook
message.

00:39:08.700 --> 00:39:15.470
If you're in studio and create 10 minutes
of work and want an eye on it, even if I can't,

00:39:15.470 --> 00:39:18.850
there is someone I know that can and will
want to.

00:39:18.850 --> 00:39:26.790
Where in Australia will you be?

00:39:26.790 --> 00:39:31.720
LEISA: In Melbourne, Victoria.

00:39:31.720 --> 00:39:36.900
I'm currently in Bremen Germany at the moment.

00:39:36.900 --> 00:39:43.170
KATE MARSH: Will you be able to access the
brilliant Caroline Bowditch?

00:39:43.170 --> 00:39:55.920
LEISA: Yes, she used to be my boss.

00:39:55.920 --> 00:40:01.720
There is a really good crip community in Melbourne.

00:40:01.720 --> 00:40:11.480
I have every confidence, got a lot of people
being able to cast a critical eye.

00:40:11.480 --> 00:40:17.340
It's so important we get to look at each other's
work and say great, or have you thought of

00:40:17.340 --> 00:40:19.360
doing it this way?

00:40:19.360 --> 00:40:28.470
KATE MARSH: I'm also a big believer it's fine
to share work with people who we know will

00:40:28.470 --> 00:40:32.800
get it to say nice things.

00:40:32.800 --> 00:40:39.640
To be clear, I want you to look and give it
a critical eye, or just because I want to

00:40:39.640 --> 00:40:50.690
share it with somebody and not for critical
feedback.

00:40:50.690 --> 00:41:03.680
Sometimes it's just good to put work out there
and know it's being seen, and say, this is

00:41:03.680 --> 00:41:05.360
how I want you to comment.

00:41:05.360 --> 00:41:15.750
I've learnt that from PhD supervision - when
someone sends you 10,000 words, it's good

00:41:15.750 --> 00:41:28.600
for them to go, go through this with a fine
toothed comb - or, be gentle.

00:41:28.600 --> 00:41:35.900
LEISA: I'm feeling so positive now, thank
you.

00:41:35.900 --> 00:41:41.920
IRENE VAN ZEELAND: I'm going to slowly bring
this to a close.

00:41:41.920 --> 00:41:45.930
Maylis if you have a last question maybe for
Kate.

00:41:45.930 --> 00:41:54.650
MAYLIS: I'd like to ask Kate something around
leadership.

00:41:54.650 --> 00:42:01.980
It was a part of the video too and I was very
interested in that.

00:42:01.980 --> 00:42:06.190
I'm the artistic director of a collective
project.

00:42:06.190 --> 00:42:12.140
We've been selected in [Proform] Europe.

00:42:12.140 --> 00:42:20.360
We are going on tour next week, to Denmark,
Sweden, France, for 10 days.

00:42:20.360 --> 00:42:27.760
We are very excited - but, it's been a journey.

00:42:27.760 --> 00:42:31.440
It's been really, really tough.

00:42:31.440 --> 00:42:36.670
I've found 3 incredible partners, programmers
in Denmark, Sweden France who were really

00:42:36.670 --> 00:42:46.350
helpful, but it's been a lot of work, admin,
emails, a lot of production work.

00:42:46.350 --> 00:42:52.860
It really put me into almost burnout last
week.

00:42:52.860 --> 00:42:58.860
I was overwhelmed by anxiety and everything.

00:42:58.860 --> 00:43:03.240
I'm an anxious person.

00:43:03.240 --> 00:43:06.420
On my daily basis it is a part of who I am.

00:43:06.420 --> 00:43:15.550
I try to embrace it as an artist, but we are
facing the fact being a leader, and anxious,

00:43:15.550 --> 00:43:26.940
and an artistic director, I question whether
I chose the right career and path, because

00:43:26.940 --> 00:43:35.690
I'm so anxious, and it's hard to be that anxious
and the leader of a project.

00:43:35.690 --> 00:43:43.170
I was asking myself a question, can we start
a conversation around being a leader and being

00:43:43.170 --> 00:43:45.750
anxious or having mental health problems?

00:43:45.750 --> 00:43:46.910
Because it's a reality.

00:43:46.910 --> 00:43:54.110
I know a lot of disabled people who deal with
anxiety on a daily basis.

00:43:54.110 --> 00:43:55.630
Where are they?

00:43:55.630 --> 00:43:57.830
Can we talk about this?

00:43:57.830 --> 00:44:01.990
I feel lonely in this situation.

00:44:01.990 --> 00:44:13.570
How can we bring anxiety and depression, and
all of this can impact our ability to send

00:44:13.570 --> 00:44:23.080
email, be on time - can we talk about this,
and where?

00:44:23.080 --> 00:44:28.881
It's part of leadership, but it's like, how
can we be leaders if we have to do the exact

00:44:28.881 --> 00:44:34.010
same thing as non disabled people, when we
don't have the same lives as non disabled

00:44:34.010 --> 00:44:36.290
people?

00:44:36.290 --> 00:44:43.540
KATE MARSH: This feels so important.

00:44:43.540 --> 00:44:46.780
Firstly, you're already doing it.

00:44:46.780 --> 00:44:47.780
You've done it.

00:44:47.780 --> 00:44:50.360
You have a 10 day tour.

00:44:50.360 --> 00:44:53.740
You're doing it.

00:44:53.740 --> 00:45:00.400
Second thing is absolutely, I feel passionately
- sorry to be the annoying person who references

00:45:00.400 --> 00:45:03.150
their own PhD.

00:45:03.150 --> 00:45:11.280
One thing I did in my PhD, credit to my supervisor,
is create a taxonomy around leadership, it

00:45:11.280 --> 00:45:19.830
was about gentle leadership, I'd love to bring
anxious leadership into that.

00:45:19.830 --> 00:45:32.400
It's alarming how even in the arts we're dominated
by a model of leadership from the top, survival

00:45:32.400 --> 00:45:34.380
of the fittest.

00:45:34.380 --> 00:45:41.420
It's so jarring with a crip or disabled experience.

00:45:41.420 --> 00:45:50.869
We really need to own those things - I'm anxious
and anxiety feeds into my leadership.

00:45:50.869 --> 00:45:54.650
I rarely go into something going, it's definitely
this.

00:45:54.650 --> 00:45:57.480
I've learnt to embrace that's my leadership
style.

00:45:57.480 --> 00:46:06.280
I like to have other people around me and
make decisions through conversation and discussion.

00:46:06.280 --> 00:46:14.970
On a practical note, that's a lot, to be an
artistic director and producer and do the

00:46:14.970 --> 00:46:19.140
administrative work.

00:46:19.140 --> 00:46:30.010
Sometimes it's inevitable - you need to acknowledge,
that's three jobs.

00:46:30.010 --> 00:46:32.730
Many artistic directors can just hand that
over.

00:46:32.730 --> 00:46:35.310
You're already doing a lot.

00:46:35.310 --> 00:46:46.540
On a practical thing I really love the way
Jess Thom, Tourettes Hero, is really open

00:46:46.540 --> 00:46:48.050
about sharing their practice.

00:46:48.050 --> 00:46:56.850
I love the way - I just worked with Jess,
who contributed to the second book I did.

00:46:56.850 --> 00:47:03.270
Real clarity in we acknowledge and work in
crip time.

00:47:03.270 --> 00:47:08.460
We don't always pick up emails between 9-5.

00:47:08.460 --> 00:47:12.890
We might cancel last minute because of needs
of people we work with.

00:47:12.890 --> 00:47:19.760
There is something that much be such a relief
- I'm getting better at doing that, there

00:47:19.760 --> 00:47:26.250
is learning about this is what I can do, and
this is where my boundary is, for example

00:47:26.250 --> 00:47:35.320
I don't answer an email, or don't want to
be in a WhatsApp group, or these are my days

00:47:35.320 --> 00:47:37.570
or rehearsal times.

00:47:37.570 --> 00:47:44.330
That takes bravery because we're pushing against
a traditional system.

00:47:44.330 --> 00:47:49.070
It's important.

00:47:49.070 --> 00:47:55.780
If we don't do that, we're setting ourselves
up to fail.

00:47:55.780 --> 00:48:06.420
If you look at the history in the arts of
disabled people in non disability led organisations

00:48:06.420 --> 00:48:10.880
undertaking leadership positions they're often
short lived.

00:48:10.880 --> 00:48:17.280
The reason I think is because the person is
exhausting themselves trying to fit into a

00:48:17.280 --> 00:48:20.930
normative pace and time of understanding work.

00:48:20.930 --> 00:48:30.430
Until we can say, no this is what leadership
looks like for me - because politics and political

00:48:30.430 --> 00:48:37.271
correctness is telling organisations they
have to involve disabled people, but if they

00:48:37.271 --> 00:48:47.330
don't respect crip time, they're setting up
to fail.

00:48:47.330 --> 00:48:57.960
There are loads of people saying - and actually
it's crip time, it is.

00:48:57.960 --> 00:49:09.040
That's such an amazing philosophy I think
we can all learn from and work within.

00:49:09.040 --> 00:49:17.820
JOE TURNBULL: In our organisation we do co-leadership
as well, co-chairs instead of having one chair

00:49:17.820 --> 00:49:23.859
we have one disabled and one non disabled
chair.

00:49:23.859 --> 00:49:28.580
Co-leadership models can be really helpful.

00:49:28.580 --> 00:49:34.930
It's quite an established practice in the
arts to have an artistic leader and a chief

00:49:34.930 --> 00:49:38.680
executive role on the practical side.

00:49:38.680 --> 00:49:43.619
There are lots of examples like that.

00:49:43.619 --> 00:49:50.940
Co-leadership can be helpful to cover each
other's bases.

00:49:50.940 --> 00:50:01.600
It's impossible for one person to have all
the attributes needed sometimes.

00:50:01.600 --> 00:50:05.109
KATE MARSH: Agreed, 100%.

00:50:05.109 --> 00:50:13.750
IRENE VAN ZEELAND: Thank you so much, thank
you Kate for sharing all these very insightful

00:50:13.750 --> 00:50:16.060
things.

00:50:16.060 --> 00:50:19.800
Don't stop quoting your own PhD, I find it
incredibly fascinating.

00:50:19.800 --> 00:50:22.270
Please continue quoting.

00:50:22.270 --> 00:50:33.109
Thank you Leisa and Maylis for joining us,
thank you Joe for organising all this, and

00:50:33.109 --> 00:50:40.369
our two that are hidden - Almudena, thank
you for setting it up, and thanks Eluned for

00:50:40.369 --> 00:50:46.170
captioning, we'll use that also in the recorded
version probably.

00:50:46.170 --> 00:50:49.710
KATE MARSH: Thanks Irene for holding us.

00:50:49.710 --> 00:50:53.240
IRENE VAN ZEELAND: It was my absolute pleasure.

00:50:53.240 --> 00:51:01.369
Thank you all for being here, I hope you walk
or roll away inspired from this conversation.

